\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For the centered Hardy-Littlewood maximal operator M over Euclidean disks in $R^2$, does every real f in $W^{1,1}(R^2)$ have M f in $W_loc^{1,1}(R^2)$ and ||gradient M $f||_L1$ $<=$ C||gradient $f||_L1$ for one absolute C?

\subsection{Short English statement}
For the centered Hardy-Littlewood maximal operator M over Euclidean disks in $R^2$, does every real f in $W^{1,1}(R^2)$ have M f in $W_loc^{1,1}(R^2)$ and ||gradient M $f||_L1$ $<=$ C||gradient $f||_L1$ for one absolute C?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] Original question source or mathematical context.
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200}.
\item[S2] OpenAI, manuscript citation page. Source of the formulation motivating this local problem.
\url{https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/preprints/An-Endpoint-Gradient-Bound-for-the-Centered-Disk-Maximal-Operator-September-26-2026/README.md}.
\end{itemize}

